summary: scripts/dev/demo.sh turns the demonstration home into a tagged, rated repository of a throwaway daemon, shown in a real GUI — to explore it, record a video, or remake the README's screenshots.

The README shows the GUI on files that look like someone's, not on test fixtures.
Those files are a home of their own, `/home/metafolder_user`: "Camille Martin", a
fictitious user, with photos of three trips, paintings, music, free films, manuals,
invoices, a cluttered downloads folder, projects with their `.git` and `__pycache__`.
Everything in it is free; its `LISEZMOI.md` says what each folder is for, its
`CRÉDITS.md` who made each file and under which licence. The home is local to the
machine it was assembled on: it is not in the repository, and the scripts below say
so and stop where it is missing (`--source` names another).

```
scripts/dev/demo.sh [--unsandboxed] gui                     # explore it, record a video
scripts/dev/demo.sh [--unsandboxed] screenshots [OUT_DIR]   # remake docs/wiki/images/demo-*.png
scripts/dev/test-demo.sh                                    # every metadata step found its files
```

!! What it builds

A daemon of its own (the fixture of [[Testing shipped scripts]]: private token
directory, shipped configuration, ephemeral port), and on it a repository named
`camille` over the home, with its database outside it (`mf repo init --metafolder`):
the home belongs to another user and stays untouched. The root is watched and
reconciled, which brings in the files and their embedded metadata
([[Embedded metadata]]): EXIF dates and GPS of the photos, artist and album of the
music.

Then `scripts/dev/demo/metadata.sh` gives the files what Camille would have: a
tag hierarchy (`place/europe/iceland`, `nature/animal/bird`,
`art/painting/impressionism`, `music/baroque`…), a denied tag (the wallpapers show
no animal), ratings and comments, in English like the README the screenshots
illustrate (the home's own file names stay French). Each of its steps writes under a
label named as she would name it — "Sort the travel photos", "Rate the favourites" — so the
[[log panel]] reads as a story and a step can be undone in front of an audience
([[Labels and contexts]]). The commands are given the daemon's port and the
repository through the environment, never on their command line, which a label
records.

Each step counts the files it names before writing: a line that finds nothing stops
the script. A home reorganised under it fails there, in `test-demo.sh`, rather than as
an empty tag nobody notices on a screenshot. The count is taken before the write, not
from it, because a write that changes nothing (a tag an earlier line already gave)
counts no record.

`metadata.sh` talks to whatever daemon `mf` reaches, so it also runs in the
demonstration account itself, against that user's own daemon, once the home is a
repository there: the way to record a video on the real desktop of that user.

!! The GUI

Both modes start a real `metafolder-gui` on a private copy of the installed
configuration, as [[Testing the GUI end to end]] does: the user's keybindings and
commands do not show.

* `gui` opens it on your display, on the repository, until its window is closed. It prints how to reach its daemon and the GUI with `mf`, to drive it during a recording.
* `screenshots` runs it on a private X server, `Xvfb`, at 1440 × 900, and builds each view of `scripts/dev/demo/views.sh` in a workspace of its own: the trips' photos as a grid beside a preview, the tag tree walked down to `nature/animal` beside the files it tags, the annotated files beside the log.

A view is built by commands and workspace variables only — no click, no key, nothing
at coordinates — so a change of style changes the picture, never what it shows. A
capture is taken once the screen stops changing, since the thumbnails load after the
commands return, for a time no duration bounds: once the repository runs no task,
then after a command (which resolves only once no panel read is in flight,
[[When a command has finished]]), when the screen has not changed for 2 s. Two
identical captures half a second apart were not enough: a slow daemon call holds
every read of the panels, the screen holds still meanwhile, and a README shipped a
grid with blank thumbnails beside a viewer still showing the previous file. The PNG
is reduced to 256 colours, a quarter of the size. `CRÉDITS.md` is copied beside the
screenshots as `CREDITS.md`: the photos they show are mostly under CC BY-SA, and the
screenshots with them.

The order of the rows is the daemon's, not sorted: a second run shows the same
photos, not necessarily in the same places.

!! Pitfalls

* The GUI reuses the id of a closed workspace. The empty workspace the GUI starts with is closed once, from the script's own shell, after the first view exists: closed from inside a view's `$(…)`, the variable saying it was closed is lost, and the next view's close takes the new workspace that got the id back.
* `workspace:send` takes a value as is: quoting it (`"'#=place'"`) sends the quotes, which the simplified grammar refuses.
* The divider between the slots can only be dragged; a drag sent with `xdotool` moves it but also selects text across both panels. The views keep the default halves.
